ndamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Joe Wilson (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Joe Wilson, a longtime Republican incumbent, seeks another term in South Carolina's 2nd District, a seat that leans Republican by about seven points. He easily secured renomination in June and holds a commanding financial advantage, with roughly $1.8 million in cash on hand against his Democratic opponent's negligible war chest. Democratic nominee Zyon Khalifa, a 25-year-old military veteran, has emphasized his local roots but has raised only about $19,000.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals, expert ratings, and prediction markets. All nine expert raters place the race at Likely R or stronger, and markets give Democrats roughly a 7% chance of an upset. Wilson is a clear favorite, though the absence of polling leaves some room for uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -9.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0576, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0526, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0819, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0438, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0396, "margin": -11.0}]}